36.2.2. Choosing a Connection
SQL statements in embedded SQL programs are by default executed on the current connection, that is,
the most recently opened one. If an application needs to manage multiple connections, then there are three
ways to handle this.
The first option is to explicitly choose a connection for each SQL statement, for example:
EXEC SQL AT connection-name SELECT ...;
This option is particularly suitable if the application needs to use several connections in mixed order.
If your application uses multiple threads of execution, they cannot share a connection concurrently. You
must either explicitly control access to the connection (using mutexes) or use a connection for each thread.
The second option is to execute a statement to switch the current connection. That statement is:
EXEC SQL SET CONNECTION connection-name;
This option is particularly convenient if many statements are to be executed on the same connection.
Here is an example program managing multiple database connections:
#include <stdio.h>
EXEC SQL BEGIN DECLARE SECTION;
    char dbname[1024];
EXEC SQL END DECLARE SECTION;
